        "title": "Part I — Low Water",
        "collapsed": false,
        "chapters": [
          {
            "id": "chap-1",
            "title": "The Brass Astrolabe",
            "subtitle": "Where the outer reef shifts overnight",
            "collapsed": false,
            "scenes": [
              {
                "id": "scene-1",
                "title": "Harbor Bell at Dawn",
                "synopsis": "Tomas inspects the tide gauge at Skerry Reach and finds an uncharted limestone shoal.",
                "status": "revision",
                "pov": "Tomas Vance",
                "location": "Skerry Reach Lighthouse",
                "timeline": "Day 1 · 05:40",
                "wordTarget": 1200,
                "content": "Before the fog bell struck five, Tomas Vance knelt beside the iron tide-well with a caliper and a salt-stained ledger. Yesterday the southern channel had measured nine fathoms at slack water.\n\nThis morning the brass plumb line stopped short at four.\n\n* * *\n\n\"Either the basin is rising,\" Clara Holm said from the lantern stair, holding an unlit storm lamp, \"or the chart room at Brinehaven lost half its ink.\"\n\nTomas wiped brine from the Margin Sextant and marked the sounding in red graphite. Out beyond the breakwater, the Bell Buoy tolled twice off-beat—an unmistakable warning that the limestone shelf had shifted again.",
                "notes": "Establish the tactile weight of Tomas's surveying tools and the uneasy friendship with Clara.",
                "snapshots": [
                  {
                    "id": "snap-1",
                    "createdAt": "2026-11-20T09:15:00.000Z",
                    "label": "First Cold Draft",
                    "content": "Tomas Vance checked the tide-well at dawn. The southern channel had fallen from nine fathoms to four overnight.",
                    "wordCount": 18
                  }
                ],
                "updatedAt": "2026-11-22T12:00:00.000Z"
              },
              {
                "id": "scene-2",
                "title": "The Chartmaker's Apprentice",
                "synopsis": "In the Brinehaven Archive, Henrik reveals a century-old survey map sealed in beeswax.",
                "status": "draft",
                "pov": "Clara Holm",
                "location": "Brinehaven Archive",
                "timeline": "Day 1 · 10:15",
                "wordTarget": 1400,
                "content": "The vaulted shelves of Brinehaven Archive smelled of cedar oil and dry kelp. Henrik Nystrom laid the cylinder onto the oak table and cracked its grey wax seal with a bone folder.\n\n\"My grandfather drew this shoreline after the winter gale of 1841,\" Henrik murmured. \"Look at the Margin Sextant bearings along the western shoal. The island didn't sink—it turned.\"",
                "notes": "Keep Henrik cautious; he distrusts the Admiralty clerks.",
                "snapshots": [],
                "updatedAt": "2026-11-22T12:15:00.000Z"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "chap-2",
            "title": "Meridian Stones",
            "subtitle": "Northward across the black kelp flats",
            "collapsed": false,
            "scenes": [
              {
                "id": "scene-3",
                "title": "Crossing the Causeway",
                "synopsis": "Tomas and Clara cross the exposed tidal road before the afternoon bore returns.",
                "status": "outline",
                "pov": "Tomas Vance",
                "location": "The Black Causeway",
                "timeline": "Day 2 · 14:30",
                "wordTarget": 1500,
                "content": "By two o'clock the mudflats steamed under pale autumn sun. Tomas slung the copper sounding reel over his shoulder while Clara checked the pocket barometer.\n\nTwo miles ahead, the first Meridian Stone jutted out of the wet basalt like a broken mast.",
                "notes": "Pace accelerates as the six-hour tidal window closes.",
                "snapshots": [],
                "updatedAt": "2026-11-22T12:30:00.000Z"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
